{"causes": ["The sudden death of Alexander the Great in 323 BCE created a power vacuum among his generals and satraps.", "The regency established by Perdiccas following Alexander's death proved unstable, leading to constant rivalries among the Diadochi.", "Antigonus's exceptional military acumen and strategic understanding enabled him to consolidate power rapidly amidst the chaos."], "effects": ["Antigonus's declaration as basileus in 306 BCE spurred other Diadochi to assume royal titles, formally dissolving Alexander's empire into independent Hellenistic kingdoms.", "His military campaigns, particularly the Battle of Ipsus, directly led to the permanent division of Alexander's empire among the victorious Diadochi.", "He founded the Antigonid dynasty, which maintained control over Macedonia and significantly influenced the Hellenistic political landscape for over a century."], "relationships": [{"sourceSlug": "antigonus-i-monophthalmus", "sourceName": "Antigonus I Monophthalmus", "verb": "CREATES", "targetSlug": "antigonid-dynasty", "targetName": "Antigonid Dynasty", "context": "Antigonus I Monophthalmus founded the Antigonid dynasty, which ruled Macedonia from 276 to 168 BCE."}, {"sourceSlug": "alexander-the-great", "sourceName": "Alexander the Great", "verb": "INFLUENCES", "targetSlug": "antigonus-i-monophthalmus", "targetName": "Antigonus I Monophthalmus", "context": "Antigonus I Monophthalmus served as a prominent general under Alexander the Great, gaining military experience crucial for his later campaigns."}, {"sourceSlug": "antigonus-i-monophthalmus", "sourceName": "Antigonus I Monophthalmus", "verb": "PARTICIPATES_IN", "targetSlug": "wars-of-the-diadochi", "targetName": "Wars of the Diadochi", "context": "Antigonus I Monophthalmus was a central figure in the Wars of the Diadochi from 322 to 301 BCE, contending for control of Alexander's empire."}, {"sourceSlug": "antigonus-i-monophthalmus", "sourceName": "Antigonus I Monophthalmus", "verb": "OCCURS_IN", "targetSlug": "phrygia", "targetName": "Phrygia", "context": "Antigonus I Monophthalmus was initially appointed satrap of Phrygia after Alexander's death, establishing his first power base."}, {"sourceSlug": "antigonus-i-monophthalmus", "sourceName": "Antigonus I Monophthalmus", "verb": "COLLABORATES_WITH", "targetSlug": "demetrius-i-poliorcetes", "targetName": "Demetrius I Poliorcetes", "context": "Antigonus I Monophthalmus collaborated extensively with his son Demetrius I Poliorcetes, who served as his leading general and successor."}], "places": [{"name": "Macedonia, Ancient Greece", "role": "Dynastic origin"}, {"name": "Phrygia, Anatolia", "role": "Initial satrapy"}, {"name": "Ipsus, Phrygia", "role": "Decisive battle"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: Antigonus I Monophthalmus", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "antigonus-i-monophthalmus"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q200401", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Antigonus_I_Monophthalmus"], "tags": ["Military Leaders & Commanders", "conflict-and-resolution", "geopolitical-linkage", "temporal-linkage"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 8, "significanceNarrative": "Antigonus I Monophthalmus was a pivotal figure whose ambition and military campaigns directly led to the formal fragmentation of Alexander's empire into distinct Hellenistic kingdoms, shaping the geopolitical map of the Eastern Mediterranean for centuries. He founded the Antigonid dynasty, which ruled Macedonia for over 100 years, profoundly influencing the region's cultural and political identity.", "significanceCategory": "continental"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-06-06T21:15:16Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#27074030672", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Antigonus I Monophthalmus was a Macedonian Greek general and successor of Alexander the Great. A prominent military leader in Alexander's army, he went on to control large parts of Alexander's former empire. He assumed the title of basileus (king) in 306 BC and reigned until his death. He was the founder of the Antigonid dynasty, which ruled over Macedonia until its conquest by the Roman Republic in 168 BC.", "newValue": "Antigonus I Monophthalmus (c. 382–300 BCE), known as 'Cyclops' due to the loss of an eye, emerged as one of Alexander the Great's most formidable Successors (Diadochi) following the Macedonian king's death in 323 BCE. His relentless ambition and military prowess were central to the turbulent Wars of the Diadochi, which fragmented Alexander's vast empire into several Hellenistic kingdoms. He founded the Antigonid dynasty, which would ultimately rule Macedonia for over a century, thereby shaping the political landscape of the Aegean and Near East for generations.

Initially appointed satrap of Phrygia by Perdiccas, Antigonus quickly consolidated power, defying the regency and engaging in a series of campaigns against other Diadochi like Lysimachus, Cassander, and Seleucus I Nicator. His major victories, such as the Battle of Paraitacene (317 BCE) and the Battle of Gabiene (316 BCE) against Eumenes of Cardia, demonstrated his strategic genius and cemented his control over Anatolia and parts of Syria. In 306 BCE, after his son Demetrius Poliorcetes' decisive naval victory at Salamis over Ptolemy I Soter, Antigonus assumed the royal title of basileus, a declaration that irrevocably signaled the end of any unified Alexandrian empire and the dawn of independent Hellenistic monarchies.

Though Antigonus's vision of a reunited Alexandrian empire under his sole rule ultimately failed at the Battle of Ipsus in 301 BCE, his actions profoundly influenced the subsequent Hellenistic world. His ambition forced the other Diadochi to declare themselves kings, formalizing the division of the empire. The Antigonid dynasty, established by him, persisted in Macedonia until its conquest by the Roman Republic in 168 BCE, providing a crucial bulwark against various incursions and maintaining a distinct Greek cultural identity in the region. His military and administrative policies laid foundational elements for later Hellenistic statecraft."}, {"timestamp": "2026-06-06T21:15:16Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#27074030672", "field": "detailsJson.causes", "oldValue": [{"title": "Classical era: flourishing of philosophical inquiry, imperial expansion, and codification of la", "type": "EventWindow", "year": "381 BCE, Elimiotis"}, {"title": "Military conflicts and strategic necessities", "type": "Idea", "year": "381 BCE, Elimiotis"}], "newValue": ["The sudden death of Alexander the Great in 323 BCE created a power vacuum among his generals and satraps.", "The regency established by Perdiccas following Alexander's death proved unstable, leading to constant rivalries among the Diadochi.", "Antigonus's exceptional military acumen and strategic understanding enabled him to consolidate power rapidly amidst the chaos."]}, {"timestamp": "2026-06-06T21:15:16Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#27074030672", "field": "detailsJson.effects", "oldValue": [{"title": "Determined military outcomes shaping geopolitical boundaries", "type": "Idea", "year": "300 BCE"}, {"title": "Contributed to enduring intellectual and institutional frameworks that shaped subsequent civili", "type": "Movement", "year": "300 BCE"}], "newValue": ["Antigonus's declaration as basileus in 306 BCE spurred other Diadochi to assume royal titles, formally dissolving Alexander's empire into independent Hellenistic kingdoms.", "His military campaigns, particularly the Battle of Ipsus, directly led to the permanent division of Alexander's empire among the victorious Diadochi.", "He founded the Antigonid dynasty, which maintained control over Macedonia and significantly influenced the Hellenistic political landscape for over a century."]}, {"timestamp": "2026-06-06T21:15:16Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#27074030672", "field": "detailsJson.relationships", "oldValue": [{"sourceSlug": "antigonus-i-monophthalmus", "sourceName": "Antigonus I Monophthalmus", "verb": "COMMANDS", "targetSlug": "field-280", "targetName": "Military Leaders & Commanders", "context": "Antigonus I Monophthalmus: Military command in military leaders & commanders"}, {"sourceSlug": "antigonus-i-monophthalmus", "sourceName": "Antigonus I Monophthalmus", "verb": "OCCURS_DURING", "targetSlug": "classical", "targetName": "Classical Era", "context": "Antigonus I Monophthalmus was active during the Classical era"}], "newValue": [{"sourceSlug": "antigonus-i-monophthalmus", "sourceName": "Antigonus I Monophthalmus", "verb": "CREATES", "targetSlug": "antigonid-dynasty", "targetName": "Antigonid Dynasty", "context": "Antigonus I Monophthalmus founded the Antigonid dynasty, which ruled Macedonia from 276 to 168 BCE."}, {"sourceSlug": "alexander-the-great", "sourceName": "Alexander the Great", "verb": "INFLUENCES", "targetSlug": "antigonus-i-monophthalmus", "targetName": "Antigonus I Monophthalmus", "context": "Antigonus I Monophthalmus served as a prominent general under Alexander the Great, gaining military experience crucial for his later campaigns."}, {"sourceSlug": "antigonus-i-monophthalmus", "sourceName": "Antigonus I Monophthalmus", "verb": "PARTICIPATES_IN", "targetSlug": "wars-of-the-diadochi", "targetName": "Wars of the Diadochi", "context": "Antigonus I Monophthalmus was a central figure in the Wars of the Diadochi from 322 to 301 BCE, contending for control of Alexander's empire."